if you could be any superhero what  superpower would you want to have I bet  you're thinking about it aren't you well  growing up believe it or not this was  something my friends and I would think  about a lot where you get really excited  and we say things like I want to fly  like Superman or I want to be able to  read 10,000 words a minute as a parent  started to listen in my answer though  was always consistent I want to make  people laugh at this point you might be  wondering how is that a superpower I  would argue that it is going up my  father was a professional comedian and I  was always fascinated because he could  get away with pretty much anything you  can ask my mom I wanted to be just like  him as it turns out I'm a business  school so that didn't quite work out but  I learned an even bigger lesson that  humor can actually teach us serious  lessons about how we can better relate  with each other  lessons I didn't quite set out to learn  in fact my dad didn't set out to be a  comedian either 20 years ago he moved  back from the United States to his home  country Ghana after studying acting in  the United States it was a pivotal time  in Ghana's history for the first time we  had democratic elections after two  decades of a military dictatorship it  was a time of a lot of excitement a lot  of hope but yet that necessary dose of  skepticism that newly democratic  countries tend to have and my father  could be pretty stubborn when he was  convinced of an idea and he was  convinced that the only way this new  democrasy would work is if we talked  openly about what was not working  so to make his point he goes on to radio  and of course the first thing he does is  publicly criticized the government as  you can imagine that didn't go down too  well public reaction to him was intense  his car was vandalized people wrote him  some pretty creative hate mail and their  sponsor has actually pulled out from the  show labeling him as overly  controversial fast forward a couple  years later he returns to his first love  which is acting so he performs in his  first one-man play originally set out to  be a drama but the audience actually  ended up laughing so much that it was  written about as one of Ghana's best  comedy plays of all time and that is how  he became a comedian why am I telling  you the story it's because in that in  that play he spoke about the exact same  issues he used to talk about on radio  this time though people drive more than  five hours line up and pay just to  listen to him that ladies and gentlemen  is the power of humor that is why I  think it's a superpower and that is why  I wanted to be just like him this  fascination with a power of humor stayed  with me through the years and actually  ended up working in the industry I  worked as a producer for a comedy show I  learned some pretty hard lessons very  quickly the most important being I'm not  funny  in fact when I told my brother that I  wanted to make people laugh for a living  he was quiet for a minute looks at me  and says I've left a lot because of you  dis Decker  so I took a different approach and I  thought to understand what it was about  humor that enables us to let our guard  down and connect so instantly and what I  found through my research and my work is  that the magic of humor is that it's  unexpected  in fact our brain works with pattern  recognition so basically when you hear a  story you subconsciously try to  autocomplete it but when the story goes  in a different direction we experience  this rush from the surprise factor humor  works because we delight in the  unexpected when it's coming from a good  place  research shows that our brains pleasure  Center actually is far more reactive to  experiences that are both pleasurable  and unexpected than experiences that are  simply pleasurable it's the reason why  surprise wedding proposals work so well  most of the time but how does this  connect us well think back to the last  time you laughed  hopefully that was a few minutes ago  think about how you felt towards the  person that made you laugh when we  experienced this rush our brain captures  and holds that emotional memory so the  next time we meet again we associate  this positive memory with the person and  warm up more quickly in other words if  you make a guy or girl laugh on the  first date you're probably gonna get a  second one so we know how humor connects  and affects us but not all of us can be  professional comedians try as we might  and as I've learned to ask myself in  business school how do we apply this  framework of using unexpectedness to  delight each other better yet how do we  scale its effect in our everyday life  and I saw you a quick story that helped  put it all together for me  a couple years ago I lost a bet to one  of my good friend Sarah  and like all our bets I had to buy her  lunch and so I'm standing in line I've  put in my order I'm ready to pay and  tell my embarrassment I've left my  wallet again and then the weirdest thing  happened a guy walks from behind me  swipes his credit card pays for an  entire meal and walks away before I can  even say thank you  and I'm standing there completely  surprised but also delighted and over  the years with similar experiences and  as I've tried to pay it forward I've  come to realize that yes we can delight  each other in small unexpected acts of  kindness think back to the last time  that you received words of encouragement  from a friend you hadn't heard from her  in a while or a friend you just made or  a time a complete stranger let you have  that first parking spot that may be a  little too much to ask in the city but  hopefully you get my point we can all  tap into what makes humor a superpower  by going a little out of our way each  day for the people around us even if  it's as simple as turning to the person  next to you and saying something  thoughtful you look nice today even in  this world  we're often encouraged to think big to  think of impact in terms of touching the  next million or billion lives yet in our  own personal lives we think we're  touched by individual moments and  individual people so my challenge for  you today is to think small will make it  unexpected you'll be pleasantly  surprised thank you  you
 

